{
  "configuration_id": "config_025",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_003 (multimodal, scgpt+imagenet_vit_l, singleprofile, concat) achieved 0.4348 acc / 0.4234 F1, while config_004 (rna_only, scgpt, singleprofile) achieved 0.4106 acc / 0.3785 F1 — a ~2.4% accuracy and ~4.5% F1 gap. This is suggestive but based on a single pair, so it is unclear whether the image modality is a strong independent signal or whether the small gain is noise/complementarity. Question: config_025 (image_only, singleprofile, imagenet_vit_l) is the clean ablation that isolates the image modality's standalone contribution while holding the image encoder and profile setting identical to config_003. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It directly tests whether the 2.4% multimodal lift is driven by a genuinely informative image channel or by a weak/noisy addition. (2) The result will sharply guide all subsequent experiments — if image_only is weak, the multimodal benefit is complementary and we should prioritize fusion/aggregation design; if image_only is competitive, we should invest in stronger image encoders or multiprofile image aggregation. (3) It changes only the model setting (multimodal→image_only) relative to config_003, satisfying the principle of isolating one design variable. (4) Under a tight budget, resolving this fundamental question about modality value is more informative than, e.g., swapping fusion to weighted (config_031) or changing the image backbone (config_015), which are secondary refinements that depend on knowing whether the image signal is worth optimizing."
}

